[[:db/retract 17592186117671 :answer-field/choices 17592186117672] [:db/retract 17592186117671 :answer-field/choices 17592186117673] [:db/retract 17592186117671 :answer-field/choices 17592186117675] [:db/retract 17592186117671 :answer-field/choices 17592186117676] [:db/retract 17592186120242 :question/answer-fields 17592186117611] [:db/retract 17592186119234 :answer-field/choices 17592186119236] [:db/retract 17592186119234 :answer-field/choices 17592186119237] [:db/retract 17592186119234 :answer-field/choices 17592186119238] [:db/retract 17592186119234 :answer-field/choices 17592186119239] {:db/id 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f08885d9-557e-5e55-b967-a7cb452a9e2a" "question-7057aab9-4ddf-5b3b-9e59-0a11a291512b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f357a402-4ed9-5871-9002-ee2ada823334" "question-7043d5b3-b126-54ab-a0bc-9816029b7532"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bc713dbc-3c1f-5a9c-8a2b-b4377300bc92" "question-86bd8ee0-d1c5-5e4f-9534-8a4e93bb860c" "question-35b2b5dc-be5b-531e-92b7-e1931e890d8a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f08885d9-557e-5e55-b967-a7cb452a9e2a" :question/id #uuid "f08885d9-557e-5e55-b967-a7cb452a9e2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127379" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}})⟨2,\\,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨2,\\,t⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨2,\\,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}(-t⟨2,t⟩+({t}^{2}+4)⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{((1)^{2}+4)}^{3/2}}⟨-2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}⟨-2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}‖⟨-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2(-1)+1(2)) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1b8a9865-39bf-545e-aeef-35a531125f5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1b8a9865-39bf-545e-aeef-35a531125f5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8a9865-39bf-545e-aeef-35a531125f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34323a95-3364-5d8b-ae6b-9b380e0bf2c9" "answer-02e39ea1-4cba-57a5-ab90-7a89782cb8d0" "answer-37ad2308-55f9-592a-86af-f156397fae09" "answer-b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" "answer-77fd4f80-1bca-57c5-b06d-9f6931a1ae23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34323a95-3364-5d8b-ae6b-9b380e0bf2c9" :answer/id #uuid "34323a95-3364-5d8b-ae6b-9b380e0bf2c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02e39ea1-4cba-57a5-ab90-7a89782cb8d0" :answer/id #uuid "02e39ea1-4cba-57a5-ab90-7a89782cb8d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37ad2308-55f9-592a-86af-f156397fae09" :answer/id #uuid "37ad2308-55f9-592a-86af-f156397fae09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{2},-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" :answer/id #uuid "b691e5f8-bc54-56bd-ba8e-fb29b5a7d2f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77fd4f80-1bca-57c5-b06d-9f6931a1ae23" :answer/id #uuid "77fd4f80-1bca-57c5-b06d-9f6931a1ae23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120252 :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\n \nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}[-2t⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+({t}^{2}+2)⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,-2{t}^{2}+4,\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{((2)^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4(2),\\,-2(2)^{2}+4,\\,4(2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}⟨8,-4,8⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{9}‖⟨2,-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2)\\cdot T(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117671 :answer-field/correct "answer-524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d439e47-3587-59b8-90e6-26a8e6ad90ab" "answer-524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" "answer-fbaf0be8-2245-53a4-8fe1-5686e5bfe610" "answer-b1c1d1e6-bb07-517c-8756-922d8ba89e65"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d439e47-3587-59b8-90e6-26a8e6ad90ab" :answer/id #uuid "3d439e47-3587-59b8-90e6-26a8e6ad90ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/id #uuid "524146c4-7e77-5f6d-af6c-ffd660ac28d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbaf0be8-2245-53a4-8fe1-5686e5bfe610" :answer/id #uuid "fbaf0be8-2245-53a4-8fe1-5686e5bfe610" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1c1d1e6-bb07-517c-8756-922d8ba89e65" :answer/id #uuid "b1c1d1e6-bb07-517c-8756-922d8ba89e65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120242 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7057aab9-4ddf-5b3b-9e59-0a11a291512b" :question/id #uuid "7057aab9-4ddf-5b3b-9e59-0a11a291512b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74850" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩,\\,t\\ge 0 \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}+t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other equation. Therefore, $P(\\sqrt{2}\\,,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1))$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨t,\\,1⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨t,\\,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩+({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-t⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9023e46d-1d99-5286-baf3-0582b7f8b0a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9023e46d-1d99-5286-baf3-0582b7f8b0a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9023e46d-1d99-5286-baf3-0582b7f8b0a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b10046fc-c183-59e3-ae38-7e794395db17" "answer-63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" "answer-a7b0f6c4-1c16-5380-8c5e-e758a32ebbc5" "answer-4b50b45b-e31f-500e-9496-cc48c7886b21" "answer-f08e5f05-4e3e-5da4-b635-cb93796cbc71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b10046fc-c183-59e3-ae38-7e794395db17" :answer/id #uuid "b10046fc-c183-59e3-ae38-7e794395db17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" :answer/id #uuid "63b699bf-e6d8-5ef0-9f70-0ede7e9e9edb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7b0f6c4-1c16-5380-8c5e-e758a32ebbc5" :answer/id #uuid "a7b0f6c4-1c16-5380-8c5e-e758a32ebbc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨-2,\\,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b50b45b-e31f-500e-9496-cc48c7886b21" :answer/id #uuid "4b50b45b-e31f-500e-9496-cc48c7886b21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f08e5f05-4e3e-5da4-b635-cb93796cbc71" :answer/id #uuid "f08e5f05-4e3e-5da4-b635-cb93796cbc71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f357a402-4ed9-5871-9002-ee2ada823334" :question/id #uuid "f357a402-4ed9-5871-9002-ee2ada823334" :question/math-academy-id "q-358780" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+12\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+12\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{5}{13}‖\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{13}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{5}{13}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f8052c5-663c-518b-bb07-10ee30f768f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f8052c5-663c-518b-bb07-10ee30f768f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8052c5-663c-518b-bb07-10ee30f768f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b" :answer/id #uuid "85f9b3c0-76af-5d80-92a4-5941254da04b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186290133 :answer-field/correct "answer-39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer/id #uuid "39d23c9a-dade-5d1b-902c-eabbc6bdf7de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186290029 :answer-field/correct "answer-999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer/id #uuid "999808ca-0444-5335-ac02-8d182fdfd0a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sin t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323659 :answer-field/correct "answer-a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/id #uuid "a87dc9d9-165f-54c0-8d1c-b67b7c6e81f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7043d5b3-b126-54ab-a0bc-9816029b7532" :question/id #uuid "7043d5b3-b126-54ab-a0bc-9816029b7532" :question/math-academy-id "q-223120" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨\\,0\\,,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-2|\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11c25002-d570-5188-8443-f9f108b5d95d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11c25002-d570-5188-8443-f9f108b5d95d" :answer-field/id #uuid "11c25002-d570-5188-8443-f9f108b5d95d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef" :answer/id #uuid "ba0cef59-7cae-59d5-aee5-dae91a214aef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bc713dbc-3c1f-5a9c-8a2b-b4377300bc92" :question/id #uuid "bc713dbc-3c1f-5a9c-8a2b-b4377300bc92" :question/math-academy-id "q-75410" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,3⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+9)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{0}{({0}^{2}+9)\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,0⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(0)\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6e89c597-7b48-520a-9aa5-d249aa23a780"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6e89c597-7b48-520a-9aa5-d249aa23a780" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e89c597-7b48-520a-9aa5-d249aa23a780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-512116d3-97dd-54be-bc16-10567d150eb8" "answer-54e8dd13-f4d3-59cf-aea8-647ea53c3240" "answer-09429f0b-5a31-576e-b53c-7a9c9089a259" "answer-4df4322e-2ca8-59b0-b25f-d74e9d345855" "answer-9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-512116d3-97dd-54be-bc16-10567d150eb8" :answer/id #uuid "512116d3-97dd-54be-bc16-10567d150eb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}⟨1,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54e8dd13-f4d3-59cf-aea8-647ea53c3240" :answer/id #uuid "54e8dd13-f4d3-59cf-aea8-647ea53c3240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09429f0b-5a31-576e-b53c-7a9c9089a259" :answer/id #uuid "09429f0b-5a31-576e-b53c-7a9c9089a259" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4df4322e-2ca8-59b0-b25f-d74e9d345855" :answer/id #uuid "4df4322e-2ca8-59b0-b25f-d74e9d345855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81" :answer/id #uuid "9b75c8af-bc89-5538-be8b-284dd95d1b81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-86bd8ee0-d1c5-5e4f-9534-8a4e93bb860c" :question/id #uuid "86bd8ee0-d1c5-5e4f-9534-8a4e93bb860c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127597" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6t}{{(1+3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{6(1)}{{(1+3(1)^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{1}{1+3(1)^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+2⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{4}q(1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(1\\cdot (-3)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{6})+3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(-3-6+9) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2dcaf563-74bd-51e5-bbaf-671c2387e005"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2dcaf563-74bd-51e5-bbaf-671c2387e005" :answer-field/id #uuid "2dcaf563-74bd-51e5-bbaf-671c2387e005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" "answer-496f3c19-e7a7-5744-b55a-b824329dc81c" "answer-a7c6b0e9-05cf-5400-9c8e-e91ea5905657" "answer-00435226-3a19-5eb9-bdc3-0cc889991ef8" "answer-a9a90765-83c0-5ed9-bd70-667b33af249d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" :answer/id #uuid "6f1effa6-46d0-5a74-afd6-d9566c6fab86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-496f3c19-e7a7-5744-b55a-b824329dc81c" :answer/id #uuid "496f3c19-e7a7-5744-b55a-b824329dc81c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-1,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7c6b0e9-05cf-5400-9c8e-e91ea5905657" :answer/id #uuid "a7c6b0e9-05cf-5400-9c8e-e91ea5905657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00435226-3a19-5eb9-bdc3-0cc889991ef8" :answer/id #uuid "00435226-3a19-5eb9-bdc3-0cc889991ef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,-1,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9a90765-83c0-5ed9-bd70-667b33af249d" :answer/id #uuid "a9a90765-83c0-5ed9-bd70-667b33af249d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}⟨-\\sqrt{6},1,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-35b2b5dc-be5b-531e-92b7-e1931e890d8a" :question/id #uuid "35b2b5dc-be5b-531e-92b7-e1931e890d8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127596" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨0,1,1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{({e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+1}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}(-⟨0,1,1⟩+2⟨0,1,0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0(0)+1(1)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be28023f-b4e1-527a-9fd6-a55af9912450"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be28023f-b4e1-527a-9fd6-a55af9912450" :answer-field/id #uuid "be28023f-b4e1-527a-9fd6-a55af9912450" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f911a805-3f13-582e-9b9e-8ec61aec91fe" "answer-625e5e39-ae98-52ef-bfcc-de3e0bace052" "answer-18b10125-03d9-569f-b489-2af1cd51651f" "answer-f33db979-e5fc-564b-9e72-04e2ed505855" "answer-e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f911a805-3f13-582e-9b9e-8ec61aec91fe" :answer/id #uuid "f911a805-3f13-582e-9b9e-8ec61aec91fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-625e5e39-ae98-52ef-bfcc-de3e0bace052" :answer/id #uuid "625e5e39-ae98-52ef-bfcc-de3e0bace052" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{2},1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18b10125-03d9-569f-b489-2af1cd51651f" :answer/id #uuid "18b10125-03d9-569f-b489-2af1cd51651f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,-2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f33db979-e5fc-564b-9e72-04e2ed505855" :answer/id #uuid "f33db979-e5fc-564b-9e72-04e2ed505855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,0,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020" :answer/id #uuid "e0ec4419-91f6-5d58-9711-3de15ef93020" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120520 :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=i,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j}{1} \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot (-j) \\\\  & =(i)\\cdot (-j) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119234 :answer-field/correct 17592186119235 :answer-field/choices ["answer-999190e0-5bbc-5bc3-8758-e312658c0541" "answer-283cc10c-c7dc-5d42-98bb-ff049bacb22b" "answer-bfdd62a5-1439-52d3-bd50-2dddd660304c" "answer-28cf7792-7abc-5ff9-b607-d63d93877d58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-999190e0-5bbc-5bc3-8758-e312658c0541" :answer/id #uuid "999190e0-5bbc-5bc3-8758-e312658c0541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-283cc10c-c7dc-5d42-98bb-ff049bacb22b" :answer/id #uuid "283cc10c-c7dc-5d42-98bb-ff049bacb22b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfdd62a5-1439-52d3-bd50-2dddd660304c" :answer/id #uuid "bfdd62a5-1439-52d3-bd50-2dddd660304c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,i-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28cf7792-7abc-5ff9-b607-d63d93877d58" :answer/id #uuid "28cf7792-7abc-5ff9-b607-d63d93877d58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i+j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121148 :tutorial/content "The **principal normal vector** to a curve $C$ at a point $P$ is a unit vector that \n\n- is perpendicular to the unit tangent vector, and\n\n- points in the direction that the curve is bending.\n\nIn general, if the unit tangent vector is given by $\\mathbf T(t),$ then the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|\\neq 0.$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that the curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos t, \\: 2\\sin t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0, \\pi].\n$$\n\nWe will compute the principal normal vector at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nFirst, using the formula for the unit tangent, $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf f'(t)}{||\\mathbf f'(t)||},$ we find that the general unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)= \\left\\langle {-\\sin t}, \\cos t\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we have the point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ The unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and the principal normal vector $\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ at this point are shown below:\n\n![](images/d0/d0a30a44bb825a8a12ca54153b6223b9bf0c2b754245b21650a5664db5e1647e.png)\n\nTo calculate the principal normal vector, we start by calculating the derivative of the unit tangent function. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general principal normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{||{T}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $\\mathbf N\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 4\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, it's easy to check that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and $\\mathbf N'(t)$ are perpendicular for all $t$ by computing the dot product and showing that we get zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121149 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ be the unit tangent vector to some differentiable vector-valued function. Since $||\\mathbf T(t)|| = 1,$ and $\\mathbf T(t)$ is obviously parallel to itself, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) = 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above relationship with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) + \\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T'(t) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t)+{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ 2{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ {T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, provided that $\\mathbf T'(t)\\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then it must be the case that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures the rate of change of $\\mathbf T(t).$ However, since $\\mathbf T(t)$ is a unit vector for all $t,$ it changes only in its direction. The vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures this rate of change in direction. \n\nTo form the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t),$ we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t),$ which leads to the definition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{||\\mathbf T'(t)||}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121150 :tutorial/content "Consider the straight line given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we attempt to calculate its principal normal vector, we will get an interesting result. First, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\begin{aligned}-2+5t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the case of a straight line, $\\mathbf T(t)$ is simply the (normalized) direction vector, as we would expect. \n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ is the zero vector, we can't normalize it as usual. If we try to apply the usual formula we get an undefined result due to division by zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{0}{‖0‖}=\\frac{0}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a straight line does not have a principal normal.\n\nThe intuition behind this result is that the principal normal points in the direction in which a curve is bending, but a straight line does not bend at all." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120013 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120641 :step/math-academy-id 626149 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120642 :step/math-academy-id 626150 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120643 :step/math-academy-id 626153 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120644 :step/math-academy-id 626156 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120645 :step/math-academy-id 626157 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120646 :step/math-academy-id 626160 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
